# Java Programming question bank
@subject Java Programming | JV | #F97316 | Core Java, OOP, exceptions, collections, threads, GUI and JDBC

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Java Basics

[EASY]
Q: Which of these is NOT a primitive data type in Java?
A) int
B) boolean
C) String
D) char
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: String is a class in java.lang; the 8 primitives are byte, short, int, long, float, double, char and boolean.

[EASY]
Q: What is the size of an int in Java?
A) 16 bits
B) 32 bits
C) 64 bits
D) Depends on the operating system
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Java fixes primitive sizes on every platform; int is always 32 bits.

[EASY]
Q: Which component converts Java source code (.java) into bytecode (.class)?
A) JVM
B) javac compiler
C) JIT compiler
D) Class loader
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What is the correct signature of the entry point of a Java program?
A) public void main(String args)
B) public static void main(String[] args)
C) static int main()
D) public main(String[] args)
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which keyword makes a variable's value unchangeable once assigned?
A) static
B) const
C) final
D) volatile
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: const is a reserved word in Java but is not used.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int x = 5;
        int y = x++ + ++x;
        System.out.println(x + " " + y);
    }
}
END
A) 7 12
B) 6 11
C) 7 11
D) 6 12
ANSWER: A
HINT: x++ uses the old value then increments; ++x increments first then uses the new value.
EXPLAIN: x++ gives 5 (x becomes 6), ++x makes x 7 and gives 7: y = 5 + 7 = 12, x = 7.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(1 + 2 + "3" + 4 + 5);
    }
}
END
A) 15
B) 3345
C) 12345
D) 339
ANSWER: B
HINT: + is evaluated left to right; once one operand is a String, + means concatenation.
EXPLAIN: 1 + 2 = 3 (numbers), then "3" + "3" = "33", then "334", then "3345".

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String a = "java";
        String b = "java";
        String c = new String("java");
        System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (a == c) + " " + a.equals(c));
    }
}
END
A) true false true
B) true true true
C) false false true
D) true false false
ANSWER: A
HINT: String literals are interned in the string pool; new String(...) always creates a new object.
EXPLAIN: a and b refer to the same pooled object; c is a different object with the same characters, so == is false but equals() is true.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer a = 127, b = 127;
        Integer c = 128, d = 128;
        System.out.println((a == b) + " " + (c == d));
    }
}
END
A) true false
B) true true
C) false false
D) false true
ANSWER: A
HINT: Autoboxing uses Integer.valueOf(), which caches a certain range of values.
EXPLAIN: Integer.valueOf caches -128..127, so a and b are the same object; 128 creates two different objects, so c == d compares two references and is false.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        byte b = (byte) 200;
        int i = b & 0xFF;
        char c = (char) ('A' + 2);
        System.out.println(b + " " + i + " " + c);
    }
}
END
A) -56 200 C
B) 200 200 C
C) -56 -56 67
D) -55 200 C
ANSWER: A
HINT: byte is signed 8-bit (range -128..127). Masking with 0xFF reinterprets the bits as unsigned.
EXPLAIN: 200 - 256 = -56; b & 0xFF gives 200 again; 'A' + 2 = 67, cast to char is 'C'.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic OOP in Java

[EASY]
Q: Which keyword is used to inherit a class in Java?
A) implements
B) extends
C) inherits
D) super
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Java does not support multiple inheritance of classes. How can a class get behaviour from several types?
A) By extending several classes
B) By implementing several interfaces
C) By using the friend keyword
D) It is impossible
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which keyword refers to the current object inside an instance method?
A) self
B) this
C) super
D) current
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: A constructor in Java:
A) Has a return type of void
B) Has the same name as the class and no return type
C) Must be static
D) Can be called like a normal method on an existing object
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which access modifier makes a member visible only inside its own class?
A) public
B) protected
C) default (no modifier)
D) private
ANSWER: D

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class A {
    A() { System.out.print("A "); }
}
class B extends A {
    B() { System.out.print("B "); }
}
class C extends B {
    C() { System.out.print("C "); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new C();
    }
}
END
A) C B A
B) A B C
C) C
D) A C
ANSWER: B
HINT: Every constructor implicitly calls super() as its first statement.
EXPLAIN: C() calls B(), which calls A(); A prints first, then B, then C.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class Animal {
    String sound() { return "..."; }
}
class Dog extends Animal {
    String sound() { return "Woof"; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal a = new Dog();
        System.out.println(a.sound());
    }
}
END
A) ...
B) Woof
C) Compilation error
D) null
ANSWER: B
HINT: Which method runs depends on the object's actual class, not the reference type.
EXPLAIN: This is dynamic method dispatch (runtime polymorphism): Dog's overriding method runs.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class Counter {
    static int total = 0;
    int mine = 0;
    Counter() { total++; mine++; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Counter a = new Counter();
        Counter b = new Counter();
        Counter c = new Counter();
        System.out.println(Counter.total + " " + c.mine);
    }
}
END
A) 3 1
B) 3 3
C) 1 1
D) 1 3
ANSWER: A
HINT: A static field is shared by all objects; an instance field belongs to each object separately.
EXPLAIN: total is incremented by all three constructors (3), but each object's own mine is 1.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class Parent {
    static String who() { return "Parent"; }
    String name() { return "Parent"; }
}
class Child extends Parent {
    static String who() { return "Child"; }
    String name() { return "Child"; }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Parent p = new Child();
        System.out.println(p.who() + " " + p.name());
    }
}
END
A) Parent Child
B) Child Child
C) Parent Parent
D) Child Parent
ANSWER: A
HINT: Static methods are hidden, not overridden; they are chosen by the reference type at compile time.
EXPLAIN: p.who() binds to Parent.who() (static, by reference type); p.name() is dispatched at runtime to Child.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class Base {
    Base() { show(); }
    void show() { System.out.print("Base "); }
}
class Derived extends Base {
    int x = 10;
    void show() { System.out.print("Derived " + x + " "); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new Derived().show();
    }
}
END
A) Derived 0 Derived 10
B) Base Derived 10
C) Derived 10 Derived 10
D) Base Base
ANSWER: A
HINT: The overridden method is called from the superclass constructor, before Derived's field initialisers run.
EXPLAIN: During Base(), show() dispatches to Derived but x has not been initialised yet (0). After construction x = 10.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Exception Handling

[EASY]
Q: Which block always executes whether or not an exception occurs?
A) try
B) catch
C) finally
D) throw
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: Which of these is a checked exception?
A) NullPointerException
B) ArithmeticException
C) IOException
D) ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: Checked exceptions extend Exception but not RuntimeException, and must be caught or declared.

[EASY]
Q: Which keyword is used in a method signature to declare that it may throw an exception?
A) throw
B) throws
C) catch
D) final
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Dividing an int by zero in Java throws:
A) NumberFormatException
B) ArithmeticException
C) IllegalStateException
D) Nothing; the result is infinity
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Integer division by zero throws ArithmeticException; floating-point division gives Infinity.

[EASY]
Q: What is the superclass of all exceptions and errors in Java?
A) Exception
B) Error
C) Throwable
D) Object only
ANSWER: C

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            int[] a = new int[2];
            a[2] = 5;
            System.out.print("A ");
        } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException e) {
            System.out.print("B ");
        } finally {
            System.out.print("C ");
        }
        System.out.print("D");
    }
}
END
A) A C D
B) B C D
C) B D
D) A B C D
ANSWER: B
HINT: Valid indices of an array of length 2 are 0 and 1.
EXPLAIN: a[2] throws before "A" is printed; the catch prints B, finally prints C, then execution continues with D.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    static int test() {
        try {
            return 1;
        } finally {
            System.out.print("finally ");
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(test());
    }
}
END
A) 1
B) finally 1
C) 1 finally
D) Compilation error
ANSWER: B
HINT: finally runs before the method actually returns to its caller.
EXPLAIN: The return value 1 is computed, finally prints, and only then main receives and prints 1.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Object s = "hello";
            Integer n = (Integer) s;
            System.out.println(n);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("NFE");
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
END
A) NFE
B) ClassCastException
C) hello
D) RuntimeException
ANSWER: B
HINT: A String object cannot be cast to Integer. Which catch block matches that exception?
EXPLAIN: The cast throws ClassCastException, which is not an NFE but is a RuntimeException, so its simple name is printed.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    @SuppressWarnings("finally")
    static int f() {
        int x = 1;
        try {
            x = 2;
            throw new RuntimeException();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            x = 3;
            return x;
        } finally {
            x = 4;
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(f());
    }
}
END
A) 3
B) 4
C) 2
D) 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: The value to return is saved when the return statement executes; finally changes the variable afterwards.
EXPLAIN: return x saves 3; finally sets the local x to 4 but does not change the already-saved return value.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
class Res implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String n;
    Res(String n) { this.n = n; System.out.print("open-" + n + " "); }
    public void close() { System.out.print("close-" + n + " "); }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Res a = new Res("a"); Res b = new Res("b")) {
            System.out.print("body ");
            throw new IllegalStateException();
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.print("caught");
        }
    }
}
END
A) open-a open-b body close-b close-a caught
B) open-a open-b body caught close-a close-b
C) open-a open-b body close-a close-b caught
D) open-a open-b body caught
ANSWER: A
HINT: Resources are closed automatically before the catch block runs, in reverse order of opening.
EXPLAIN: try-with-resources closes b then a (reverse order) and only then hands the exception to catch.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Collections & Generics

[EASY]
Q: Which collection does NOT allow duplicate elements?
A) ArrayList
B) LinkedList
C) HashSet
D) Vector
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: Which interface stores data as key-value pairs?
A) List
B) Set
C) Map
D) Queue
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: Which list gives O(1) access by index?
A) ArrayList
B) LinkedList
C) Both equally
D) Neither
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: ArrayList is backed by an array; LinkedList must walk the nodes.

[EASY]
Q: Which collection keeps its keys in sorted order?
A) HashMap
B) TreeMap
C) LinkedHashMap
D) Hashtable
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: TreeMap is a red-black tree that orders keys by natural order or a Comparator.

[EASY]
Q: Generics in Java mainly provide:
A) Faster execution
B) Compile-time type safety
C) Multiple inheritance
D) Automatic memory management
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(10, 20, 30, 40));
        list.remove(1);
        list.remove(Integer.valueOf(40));
        System.out.println(list);
    }
}
END
A) [10, 30]
B) [20, 30]
C) [10, 20, 30]
D) [30, 40]
ANSWER: A
HINT: remove(int) removes by index, remove(Object) removes by value.
EXPLAIN: remove(1) removes index 1 (20); remove(Integer 40) removes the value 40.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<String> s1 = new TreeSet<>(Arrays.asList("pear", "apple", "fig", "apple"));
        Set<String> s2 = new LinkedHashSet<>(Arrays.asList("pear", "apple", "fig", "apple"));
        System.out.println(s1 + " " + s2);
    }
}
END
A) [apple, fig, pear] [pear, apple, fig]
B) [pear, apple, fig] [apple, fig, pear]
C) [apple, apple, fig, pear] [pear, apple, fig]
D) [apple, fig, pear] [apple, fig, pear]
ANSWER: A
HINT: Both remove the duplicate. TreeSet sorts; LinkedHashSet remembers insertion order.
EXPLAIN: TreeSet orders alphabetically; LinkedHashSet keeps first-insertion order.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new TreeMap<>();
        for (char c : "banana".toCharArray()) {
            freq.put(c, freq.getOrDefault(c, 0) + 1);
        }
        System.out.println(freq);
    }
}
END
A) {a=3, b=1, n=2}
B) {b=1, a=3, n=2}
C) {a=3, n=2, b=1}
D) {a=1, b=1, n=1}
ANSWER: A
HINT: Count each character; a TreeMap prints keys in sorted order.
EXPLAIN: banana has a x3, n x2, b x1; sorted keys give {a=3, b=1, n=2}.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>(Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4));
        try {
            for (Integer x : list) {
                if (x == 2) list.remove(x);
            }
            System.out.println(list);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            System.out.println(e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }
    }
}
END
A) [1, 3, 4]
B) ConcurrentModificationException
C) [1, 2, 3, 4]
D) IndexOutOfBoundsException
ANSWER: B
HINT: The for-each loop uses an Iterator, which is fail-fast.
EXPLAIN: Removing directly from the list changes its modCount; the iterator's next call detects this and throws ConcurrentModificationException. Use Iterator.remove() or removeIf instead.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static class Key {
        int id;
        Key(int id) { this.id = id; }
        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Key && ((Key) o).id == id; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<Key> set = new HashSet<>();
        set.add(new Key(1));
        set.add(new Key(1));
        Key k = new Key(1);
        System.out.println(set.size() + " " + k.equals(new Key(1)));
    }
}
END
A) 2 true
B) 1 true
C) 2 false
D) 1 false
ANSWER: A
HINT: HashSet first compares hashCode() to find the bucket, then equals(). hashCode() was not overridden.
EXPLAIN: Without hashCode(), the two keys have different identity hashes and land in different buckets, so both are stored even though equals() says they are equal.

# =====================================================================
@subtopic Multithreading

[EASY]
Q: Which two ways can be used to create a thread in Java?
A) Extending Thread or implementing Runnable
B) Extending Runnable or implementing Thread
C) Using the thread keyword
D) Only by using ExecutorService
ANSWER: A

[EASY]
Q: Which method starts a new thread of execution?
A) run()
B) start()
C) execute()
D) begin()
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Calling run() directly just runs the method in the current thread.

[EASY]
Q: The synchronized keyword is used to:
A) Make code run faster
B) Allow only one thread at a time into a block/method locked on the same object
C) Create a new thread
D) Stop a thread
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which method makes the current thread wait until another thread finishes?
A) sleep()
B) yield()
C) join()
D) notify()
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: In which state is a thread after it is created with new Thread(...) but before start() is called?
A) RUNNABLE
B) NEW
C) BLOCKED
D) TERMINATED
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Thread t = new Thread(() -> System.out.print("worker "));
        t.run();
        System.out.print(Thread.currentThread().getName());
    }
}
END
A) worker main
B) main worker
C) worker Thread-0
D) The order is unpredictable
ANSWER: A
HINT: Is a new thread actually started here?
EXPLAIN: run() is called directly, so the lambda runs synchronously in the main thread before the name is printed.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    static int count = 0;
    static synchronized void inc() { count++; }
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread a = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) inc(); });
        Thread b = new Thread(() -> { for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) inc(); });
        a.start(); b.start();
        a.join(); b.join();
        System.out.println(count);
    }
}
END
A) 2000
B) A number between 1000 and 2000
C) 1000
D) 0
ANSWER: A
HINT: Is the increment protected, and does main wait for both threads?
EXPLAIN: inc() is synchronized, so no update is lost, and join() waits for both threads: always 2000.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Two threads each run count++ 100000 times on a shared non-volatile int field WITHOUT synchronization. Why can the final value be less than 200000?
A) count++ is a read-modify-write of three steps, so updates can be lost (race condition)
B) Java limits int fields to 100000
C) Threads cannot share fields
D) The JVM runs only one thread at a time
ANSWER: A
HINT: Break count++ into the machine-level steps it needs.
EXPLAIN: Two threads can both read the same old value and both write old + 1, losing one increment.

[HARD]
Q: Which statement about wait() and sleep() is correct?
A) wait() releases the object's monitor lock; sleep() keeps any locks it holds
B) sleep() releases all locks; wait() keeps them
C) Both release locks
D) wait() can be called without holding the lock
ANSWER: A
HINT: Think about why wait() must be called inside a synchronized block.
EXPLAIN: wait() must be called while holding the monitor and releases it until notified; Thread.sleep() just pauses and keeps every lock held.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
        Future<Integer> f1 = pool.submit(() -> 6 * 7);
        Future<Integer> f2 = pool.submit(() -> f1.get() + 8);
        System.out.println(f2.get());
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
END
A) 50
B) 42
C) 8
D) The program deadlocks
ANSWER: A
HINT: Future.get() blocks until that task has finished. With two worker threads, can both tasks progress?
EXPLAIN: f1 computes 42; f2 waits for it and returns 50. With 2 threads there is no deadlock (with 1 thread it would also finish because f1 is queued first).

# =====================================================================
@subtopic GUI (AWT/Swing) & JDBC

[EASY]
Q: Which package contains the Swing components such as JFrame and JButton?
A) java.awt
B) javax.swing
C) java.swing
D) java.gui
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which listener interface handles a button click in Swing/AWT?
A) MouseMotionListener
B) ActionListener
C) KeyListener
D) WindowListener
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which layout manager arranges components in five regions: NORTH, SOUTH, EAST, WEST and CENTER?
A) FlowLayout
B) GridLayout
C) BorderLayout
D) CardLayout
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: In JDBC, which interface is preferred for running parameterised SQL and prevents SQL injection?
A) Statement
B) PreparedStatement
C) ResultSet
D) DriverManager
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which method of DriverManager opens a database connection?
A) openConnection()
B) getConnection()
C) connect()
D) createConnection()
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: Why should long-running work (e.g. a slow database query) NOT run inside a Swing button's ActionListener?
A) ActionListeners cannot access databases
B) Listeners run on the Event Dispatch Thread, so the UI freezes until the work finishes
C) Swing automatically cancels long listeners
D) It causes a compilation error
ANSWER: B
HINT: Which single thread both runs listeners and repaints the screen?
EXPLAIN: Swing is single-threaded; blocking the EDT stops repainting and input. Use SwingWorker or a background thread.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which layout manager is best for switching between several screens (panels) in the same window, one visible at a time?
A) FlowLayout
B) GridBagLayout
C) CardLayout
D) BoxLayout
ANSWER: C
HINT: Think of a deck where only the top card shows.
EXPLAIN: CardLayout stacks components and shows one at a time; this quiz application uses it for its screens.

[MEDIUM]
Q: After executing a SELECT with a PreparedStatement, how do you iterate over the returned rows?
A) while (rs.hasNext()) { ... }
B) while (rs.next()) { ... }
C) for (Row r : rs) { ... }
D) rs.forEach(...)
ANSWER: B
HINT: The cursor starts BEFORE the first row.
EXPLAIN: ResultSet.next() moves the cursor forward and returns false when there are no more rows.

[HARD]
Q: In JDBC, a transfer must debit one account and credit another atomically. Which sequence is correct?
A) setAutoCommit(false); run both updates; commit(); on SQLException call rollback()
B) Run both updates with auto-commit on; call rollback() if the second fails
C) setAutoCommit(true); commit(); then run updates
D) Use two separate connections, one per update
ANSWER: A
HINT: With auto-commit on, each statement is committed the moment it runs.
EXPLAIN: Turning auto-commit off groups both statements in one transaction that is either committed together or rolled back together.

[HARD]
Q: In this Swing snippet, what text does the label show after the button is clicked three times?
CODE java
JLabel label = new JLabel("0");
JButton button = new JButton("Add");
int[] count = {0};
button.addActionListener(e -> {
    count[0] += 2;
    label.setText(String.valueOf(count[0]));
});
END
A) 6
B) 3
C) 2
D) 0, because local variables cannot change inside a lambda
ANSWER: A
HINT: The array reference is effectively final, but its contents can still change.
EXPLAIN: Lambdas can capture only effectively-final variables; using a one-element array is a common workaround. Each click adds 2: 2, 4, 6.
